<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ageladas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 520–460 BCE</span> · Also known as Hageladas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ageladas, also spelled Hageladas, was a Greek bronze sculptor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> active in the late 6th and early 5th centuries BCE. He made statues of Olympic victors and of gods, among them a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messenians">Messenians</a> at Ithome. Later tradition named him the teacher of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phidias">Phidias</a>, Myron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polykleitos">Polykleitos</a>, though the chronology makes this doubtful.</p></div>
